## 6. Security Considerations

- **Minimal attack surface.** Only one public write endpoint exists (order
  submission). Catalog/blog reads are public and read-only.
- **Server-side secrets.** Credentials used to deliver to the notification sink
  live only on the server; they are never shipped to the browser. (Which
  credentials exactly is a TSD concern.)
- **Idempotent submission.** The write endpoint dedupes by cart id within a short
  window so a double click or a retry does not produce two messages.
- **No inbound from the sink.** The notification channel is write-only from the
  system's point of view; nothing is read back from it into the storefront.
- **PII minimization + consent.** The order form collects only name + contact +
  the order. Submission is blocked until explicit consent is given (Ley 1581).
  Channel access is restricted to Shirley. (Full privacy treatment in the PRD
  §3.6.)
- **No LLM attack surface.** There is no model invocation in the runtime path;
  prompt-injection, token-abuse, and cost-blowup risks do not exist for v3.0.
  They return — with mitigations — at agentic level L1+.
- **Public endpoint abuse (residual risk).** Order submission is public; a
  malicious actor could spam the channel. Mitigation: idempotency + (deferred)
  rate limiting. Tracked as a known gap.

